.th PC I 4/1/77
.sh NAME
pc \*- Pascal compiler (generating interpreter code)
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd pc
[
\fB\*-b\fRn\fBlmpstuwz\fR
] file
.sh DESCRIPTION
This document is a quick reference to
.it pc
usage.
The full reference to the implementation is the UNIX Pascal
``Report Appendix.''
For an introduction and more explanation see the UNIX Pascal
``User Manual.''
.s3
.it Pc
compiles the program in \fIfile\fR leaving interpreter code
in the file \fIobj\fR to be interpreted by
.it "pcx (I)."
The argument \fIfile\fR must end with `.p' to indicate that it is
a Pascal source program.
.s3
The following flags are interpreted by 
.it pc.
The options affected by these flags on the command line can also
be controlled in comments within the program text.  See the ``User Manual''
and the ``Report Appendix'' for details.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-b\fRn	\fIN\fR is a single digit and
specifies the buffering for the file \fIoutput\fR.
By default this option has the value 1 which causes \fIoutput\fR to be
line buffered.  When given the value 0, \fIoutput\fR is
unbuffered, and with value 2 or greater \fIoutput\fR is block buffered.
See the ``User Manual'' and the ``Report Appendix'' for details.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-l\fR	Causes the compiler to generate a listing on the standard
output.  The default is off.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-m\fR	Allows execution profiling with 
.it "pxp (I)"
by generating statement counters.
See the ``PXP User Manual'' for details.
The default is off.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-p\fR	Causes generation of
a control backtrace on error.
Prevents infinite loops by enforcing a statement count limit.
The default is on.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-s\fR	Causes the compiler to accept standard Pascal only.  The
default is off.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-t\fR	Forces generation of additional runtime tests to check all
assignments
to variables of subrange type and subrange value parameters
to insure that the value lies in the
specified range.
Forces generation of code for \fIassert\fR statements,
which are otherwise treated as comments. 
The default is on.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-u\fR	Causes the compiler to restrict the number of relevant
characters in each source line to 72.  The default is off.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-w\fR	Causes some warning diagnostics to be suppressed.  See the
section on ``DIAGNOSTICS'' below for a list of the warnings in this class.
The default is off.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-z\fR	Is a synonym for the \fB\*-m\fR option.
.i0
.dt
.sh "FILES"
/lib/pcstrngs	contains the text of the error messages
.br
obj		receives the interpreter code
.sh "SEE ALSO"
UNIX Pascal ``User Manual,'' ``Report Appendix,'' and ``PXP User Manual,''
pcx (I),
pxp (I),
pcc (I),
pxref (I)
.sh "DIAGNOSTICS"
Syntactic error messages are interspersed in the text with
a ``---^---'' flag indicating the relevant place.
If the listing is not enabled, then a listing of each line with a syntactic
error message is forced.
Parsing is currently done by a modified
.it "yacc (I)"
LALR parser and syntactic error recovery is not good.
Syntactic error messages should therefore be taken with a grain of salt.
.s3
Semantic error messages include a relevant line number and
are currently given
at the end of \fIunits\fR in the program,
where \fIunits\fR 
correspond to major chunks of the program
such as a \fIprocedure\fR body or \fIvar\fR declaration part.
Semantic error messages are intended to be self-explanatory.
Reference to a Pascal report may prove helpful.
.s3
The first character of each message indicates its class - `w' for non-fatal
warnings, `s' for non-standard Pascal constructs, and `E' for fatal errors.
Some warnings are \fIsuppressible\fR and 
do not necessarily reflect errors in the source
program, but indicate that while the program is correct Pascal,
it somehow seems dubiously correct.
Warnings in this class include variables which
are defined and never used, variables which are referenced without ever
being assigned to and comments within comments.
.sh "AUTHORS"
Charles Haley, William Joy, and Ken Thompson
.sh BUGS
The statement ``\fIif [ ] = [ ] then\fR'' evokes a strange diagnostic.
.s3
Syntactic error recovery is very flakey.
.s3
The semantics of \fIpacked\fR are not implemented.
.s3
No detection of assignment to variables bound because they control
\fIwith\fR's.
.s3
Formal parameters which
are \fIprocedure\fR or \fIfunction\fR are not supported.
.s3
The procedures \fInew\fR and \fIdispose\fR with more
than one argument are not supported.
The extra arguments are completely ignored, and all records have the size
of the largest variant.
.s3
The functions
.it column,
.it line,
and
.it pageline
are unimplemented.
